// Match-report letters from F.W. Trumper, chronicler of Alcohology CC
// (University of York Tin Pot 20/20 League). Extracted from the original
// Word documents circulated by email.
export const trumperLetters = [
  {
    "id": "physics",
    "title": "Victory Over Physics",
    "subtitle": "A record 173-1, and a first win at last",
    "summary": "After a winless first half of the season, Alcohology CC posts a record 173-1 and demolishes Physics by 104 runs, celebrated with Ultimate Burgers and Darling bingo.",
    "source": "trumper-Physics.doc",
    "paragraphs": [
      "In the dim mists of prehistory, before even I was around to document the goings-on of the world, news was a misnamed commodity. For, though a piece of information might be new to whoever received it, it was frequently far from ‘new’ in any immediate sense of its having happened recently, by virtue of its transit via pigeon, donkey, word-of-mouth and eves-dropping. Even when I was a youngster, the doings of our beflannelled heroes on foreign wickets would take several days to arrive, before the widespread adoption of a cunning combination of electricity and cat’s whiskers allowed the anxious masses an immediate appraisal of the latest imperial humiliation at the hands of our soon to be emancipated opponents. Such tardy delivery of information in the days before mass communications in no way excuses my recent silence on the subject of Alcohology CC, but makes for a cheerfully diverting aside nonetheless.",
      "The last few months have been dispiriting, to say the least. I shall not describe in any detail our defeats to Chemistry (where we lost a match played in virtually aquatic conditions) Smith and Nephew (it rained, but we played on), PDP (no comment is perhaps best) and Psychology (drizzle, a cold wind and fog patches, rain later, moderate to poor. Now for the reports from coastal stations …). This last encounter had seen our batsmen score a record 155, falling short of the required total by a mere 4 runs. The trauma of this defeat would destroy lesser sides, but not Alcohology, as we have become rather used to it. In every game we gave of our best, naturally, but although our bowlers toiled grittily and our batsmen showed flashes of true class and often played brilliantly, we found ourselves continually undone by the heroics of a single man, with ten others huddled gratefully in his all-conquering shadow. Perhaps it is better to have one Titan to batter all before you, but this is not the Alcohology way. Unfortunately, it seems to be the way of at least 5 of the 10 teams in our league. Thus it was that we arrived at the half-way mark of the season without a single point, partly due to our noble and honourable willingness to play when other, lesser teams are happy to take a point for a rain-enforced draw, but mostly due to our inability to beat anyone.",
      "The battle-scarred veterans who took the field against Physics were indicative of this season’s trials. The Maverick’s knees were held together with gaffer-tape and optimism. The Cockney Rebel was nursing a damaged hamstring. The Gangler’s back, knee, elbow and mind had all seen better days. The Terrier had only just hobbled back into action following his surprising pre-season ankle injury – surprising not because of the timing but because it was sustained playing cricket, rather than making a sandwich or moving a Christmas tree. There were several notable absentees. The Little Master had slipped whilst modelling some brogues for the Harrods’s Winter Collection and was unable to play. The Berserker had hurt his back terrifying people in the trenches. Indeed, everywhere one looked, one saw the debris of warfare. Even those of us who were physically unharmed bore the unmistakably haunted look of mental torture. I shall never forget the Demon’s hollow stare and pinched, anxious face as long as I live, whilst even the normally sanguine Timbers wore the shell-shocked visage of an earthquake survivor, although it may have also have been indigestion.",
      "Our cause was considerably bolstered, however, by the effervescent beard of Dr Tweak, which bristled as ever with static and picked up the World Service whenever he face SSE. The charged atmosphere affected all of us wearing man-made fibres, with Mr Charno seeming very pleased to have the opportunity to don his favourite neoprene wicket-keeping pants. Slight was this joy, however, compared to the presence in our midst of Robin, our latest middle-order sensation, resplendent in his Lincoln-green tights, which I must say showed off his neatly turned ankles to great effect.",
      "Captain Arnott, fresh from his convincing return to action against Psychology, during which he took four wickets and immense umbrage at their captain’s refusal to walk when clearly caught behind, strode to the middle and promptly lost the toss. We were invited to bat, and the Terrier came back from the middle with the air of someone who had a cunning plan and had just enacted it, which we, in our state of defeatist dejection, took merely to be embittered resignation. Inscrutable as ever, the Elfin One lit a roll-up and sent Maverick and the Growbag into the fray.",
      "The Maverick’s expression upon returning after holing out on 28 was easier to read. It was the look of a man who has seen great riches and blown it before he could cash in. For although some of the bowling was good, even bordering on the edge of tricky, it was clear that our opposition lacked great store of strength in depth. This was aptly demonstrated by an over which took about 10 minutes to bowl and included at least 7 no balls and 5 wides, causing a sweating Pat to scribble furiously all over the scorebook and much relief when the umpire turned a blind eye to the last ball as it flew high over Charno’s head and pitched somewhere near Selby.",
      "In the midst of this erratic bedlam, Hellewell and the Changonator got to work. Once again, we were blessed with a charming medley of classical pulls and drives, and arrestingly agricultural bludgeoning. Both proved equally effective, as our score raced along. It crossed the Rubicon of 100, that unattainable dream of a few years ago, and sailed by 120 before the 15th over. The ACC all-time scoring record fell soon after, and the innings came to an end in a blaze of boundaries which saw Mr Hellewell make 54, Mr Charno make 60, and the total amassed a record 173-1. Captain Arnott was at great pains to establish the accurate total, repeating it endlessly with an ecstatic smile across his dazed face. I learn from a source that he was still murmuring it late into the night, and may indeed still be whispering it quietly to himself as you read this. 173-1. 173-1.",
      "173-1.",
      "Confident of victory, but mindful of the fate of those who fail to strike barn doors with musical instruments from close range, we took to the field determined to press home our advantage. There were grounds for caution. We hadn’t won a single game since last year, and our opponents’ team name applied more accurately to us than to them. There was every chance that the Maverick’s knee-caps could end up rolling past square-leg and away for a boundary. It was entirely possible that the Gangler would dislocate a shoulder in mid-delivery and end up strangling himself with a flailing limb. Charno’s beloved neoprene keeping-pants could split asunder at any moment, causing radio interference as far away as Belgium. Some teams, after a run as bad as ours, can blow even the most promising of opportunities. Some teams, after a string of defeats, cannot cope with the pressure of impending victory. Some teams, faced with a moderate challenge after a season of devastating toil, buckle under the final straw.",
      "Not our team. We looked Victory in the eyes, invited her to supper one evening next week, and gathered her manfully in our arms as the orchestra played late into the night.",
      "And we bowled rather well, too. Mitchell opened from the Shed end, causing immediate difficulty to their openers, and thankfully retained both patellas at the end of the first over. Milsted started from the Golf Course end, and soon found a good length that proved conducive for a maiden. The first wicket fell in Mitchell’s next over, as the batsman spooned a catch to mid-on and sent a shudder of panic reverberating through their entire batting order. In his second over, Milsted struck with a nasty seaming delivery that evaded a despairing waft and planted a Glasgow kiss halfway up off-stump. Already 4-2, it seemed the game could be over in very short order.",
      "The next batsman was faced with the eager, searching nose of the Terrier, unleashed early to try and recapture the sparkling form of last week. His first ball went for 4. For a brief moment it looked as if resurgence was imminent. The batsman saw off Arnott, getting to the other end to have a go at the Gangler, whose saw the first two balls of his third over go for 3 each. We felt the cold breath of failure waft over the outfield, and disquiet rumbled around the team, although it could have been indigestion. Happily, however, Milsted chose this moment to bowl his mystery, once-a-season Yorker (a mystery to him as much as to anyone else) and removed the middle stump. This was the cue for wild and gay celebration, the result of which was a part each for Arnott and Milsted in the new Broadway production of Billy Elliot and a traditional manly hand-shake all round.",
      "With a twinkle of glee in his eyes and his ball cupped firmly in hand, Captain Arnott steamed in for another three overs, during which he removed three batsmen. There is simply no adequate response to bowling of that calibre, and by the end of his spell, the Helperby Hurricane had crushed the Physics middle-order for the measly price of a mere 8 runs. With the vultures circling overhead and Death noisily sharpening his scythe behind the sightscreen, our spin bowlers took over and attacked the wounded beast like a pack of ravenous hyenas. Timbers showed masterly control of an increasingly greasy ball and took 1-19. The Demon bowled 2 overs of sulphurous intent and was unlucky not to strike, although he did tempt the hapless number 8 into a walk which was punished by some superb Nylon glovework. Robin of Loxley tormented the tail with brutal accuracy and was similarly unfortunate, although he had all and sundry scratching their heads trying to set a field for left-arm around the wicket, delivered by running from the on-side and in front of the umpire to bowl. The result was a positively balletic dance of whirling arms and shuffling feet that upstaged Arnott and Milsted’s earlier antics by securing the part of Billy Elliot himself. It is for this reason that Robin has unfortunately played his last game for ACC, and we wish him well in his new theatrical career.",
      "The final word went to Dr Tweak, whose two overs were a master-class in miserly menace, and who by taking the final wicket ended with figures of 1-1, with Physics dismissed for 69 and the margin of victory a convincing 104 runs. Always graceful in victory, our celebrations were muted but none the less joyous for it, and we departed for Ultimate Burgers, congratulations and Darling bingo. The Maverick won this last contest, scoring an unassailable 97 following the late arrival of his meal and winning a bowl of potato wedges in the process: a fitting end to a magnificent evening of Corinthian endeavour.",
      "Some may point out that victory against a weak team with only 10 players is less meaningful than a hard-fought, close contest against a worthy foe, with the result in doubt until the final delivery amid genuine and profound excitement. They may have a point, and it would be truly noble to accept that our 4 run defeat against Psychology was a more meaningful, soul-affirming, and wholesome experience that left us wiser, if sadder, human beings, than the comfortable and perhaps slightly embarrassing demolition we inflicted on Physics last Friday. Indeed, many of us were of this opinion after the game and said as much afterwards.",
      "But be that as it may. We sodding well won, and about time too.",
      "Yours,",
      "FWT"
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "season-close",
    "title": "Plonge, Biols, Heavy Rollers and Out",
    "subtitle": "A season's close amid fog and record partnerships",
    "summary": "The Changonator retires on 101 against Plonge, Biology are strangled in the 90s, and a record 153-0 opening stand against Heavy Rollers ends the season in impenetrable fog.",
    "source": "trumper-Plonge, Biols, Heavy Rollers and out.doc",
    "paragraphs": [
      "Chaps,",
      "As time deposits a thin film of wistfully nostalgic dust over this season’s Alchology exploits, it once again falls to your elderly scribe to cast his seed over the virgin white meadow of the page, and sit back in anticipation of what may arise.",
      "May I firstly offer my deep and sincere thanks to our skipper for his regular match reports, without which many of the team’s eager followers would have remained entirely ignorant of such thrilling encounters such as the 4-run defeat by Smith and Nephew, or the equally narrow victory over Plonge. Captain Arnott’s prompt, exhilarating and occasionally accurate missives have gallantly filled the breech left by my tardy efforts. Let it be known that my silences are far from wilful; rather, I am terribly, terribly old, and I am wont to forget where I am or what I am doing for weeks at a stretch. Many a time lately have I suddenly realised where I am, startling everyone around me, as my otherwise capable body seems to have carried on without the guiding counsel of my crumbling mind. Thus it was that I hurled an entire case of vodka clean through an elegantly constructed display of new-flavoured crisps in Tescos – my last conscious knowledge of my whereabouts having been a long bath of a fortnight previous. I came to clutching said refreshments and was immediately concerned for my modesty. I remain indebted to the young lady whose quick-thinking deployment of a life-size cardboard cut-out of Gary Lineker spared me a charge of indecent exposure. There are only so many one can commit before they restrain one’s movements.",
      "My bewilderment and erratic behaviour, and periodic silences, may therefore be easily understood.",
      "So it is that have no recollection whatsoever of our thrilling victory over Plonge, where we won by 10 runs, having witnessed the Changonator thunder 101 and gracefully retire his innings. This magnanimous act of charity, so applauded by all and sundry, was nearly transformed into an act of bitter folly when their opener replied with 102. However, thanks to the magnificent Hellewell, blushes were spared and MBEs did not have to be returned. My source informs me that his masterful delivery of the final over may go down in history as a moment worthy of champagne celebration. Needing 18 off 6 balls, the first was thumped for 6, but the second was spooned into Growbag’s large and grateful hands. The remaining 4 balls fall into the historical category reserved for insignificant detail and may infuriate scholars of several centuries hence by not meriting description; that is their problem and not ours. We won. No amount of revisionist argument will alter that.",
      "If only I could remember it.",
      "I am equally ignorant of our subsequent victory over Biology. This side have proven mighty opponents in seasons past, regularly unleashing carnage on our bowling attack, except for the memorable game several years ago when they bowled us out for 90-odd and we defended it, leaving them 3 short after the final, Tweak-tastic over. This time, the sides were more evenly matched than previously, and some determined gritty stuff from the opening duo Mitchell and Hellewell followed by a pyrotechnic cameo from the Polyester Prince set a target of 123. I am told that champagne recognition may be in order for the Berserker’s fine innings – a four courtesy of bizarrely poor fielding, and the following delivery conjuring a superb and unrepeatable catch. My two budgerigars ‘Sublime’ and ‘Ridiculous’ are shuffling in appreciation on their perches as I write.",
      "Our bowlers set to work; the Berserker gaining full marks for effort and attaining Jimmy Anderson-like levels of misfortune in remaining wicketless. Continuing the England theme, Captain Arnott described his own bowling performance as ‘Harmison-esque’, insofar as third slip needed a helmet and the stumps remained blissfully untroubled. Ultimately, however, thanks to a superb fielding performance and contributions all round, the Biols were strangled in the 90s by the close of proceedings.",
      "Not a single moment of it remains in my memory, and yet there are men of the cloth prepared to swear that I was there. I do not doubt it. I do not, however, remember it.",
      "Thankfully, my memory had returned in time for the final game of the season. Having fortunately escaped censure following the Tesco incident, I was free to witness the re-arranged fixture against the Heavy Rollers. The original date had fallen victim to the now traditional July monsoon; unfortunately the customary August downpours threatened to strike the same death-blow to the season’s curtain-closer.",
      "The Gangler was the first to arrive at the resolutely locked pavilion, resplendent in full waterproofs. The anticipated pleasure of the first-team wicket was somewhat marred by the necessity to swim to it, and by the lack of shelter available. However, optimism is a powerful force, and by the time the rest of the side had arrived Milsted was stripped down to thermals and cheerfully declaring the storm to be on its way out. By the law of averages he has to be correct at least once a decade, and this occasion was it. He waded back from the covered pitch, pulling seaweed from his trunks and announced that the wicket was dry.",
      "And so it was. Nothing else, however, could match that description. Undeterred, we informed our somewhat reluctant opponents that the pub would have to be postponed, and sent Mitchell out in a dingy to perform the toss. He duly won, chose to bat and paddled out to the middle with Mr Hellewell.",
      "What came next was majestic, awe inspiring and even beautiful, in a brutal way. The first over went for 12. The second was dispatched for 8. Even when the bowlers settled down, the scoring rate stayed murderously high. The deadly blade belonged to the Maverick, who carved, smote, swatted and stroked his way to 50 in a fashion that brought tears to an old connoisseur’s eyes. At the other end, the Growbag took his time, supporting his senior partner admirably and displaying an uncanny knack to hammer the ball straight to a fielder for a single. So proficient at this was he that at one point his score-book tally looked more like a bar-code. No matter. At 10 overs the score was 78-0. The rain rallied itself for a final assault on proceedings, necessitating a brief interlude; after this both men set to with renewed gusto. Arnott has brilliantly described the final over; all I can add is that the sight of Mr Hellwell at the non-striker’s end on 49 with 3 balls to go caused great agony and consternation, which was only temporarily relieved by a quick single, only to be catastrophically heightened by a beautiful block from the imminent half-centurion. It transpired, however, that he knew exactly what he was doing and, as an experienced showman, he was merely acting through a pre-ordained routine. With the half-smile and flourish that has graced most of the great stages of the empire, he leaned gracefully into a cut and ran two, securing his 50 and bringing up a score of 153-0. This later transpired to be a record for an opening pair in the league; another candidate, perhaps for champagne recognition. Captain Arnott was especially pleased, for Mr Hellewell, for the team, and also for his bat, which in the hands of the aforementioned hero had finally scored some runs.",
      "Our opponents seemed downheartened, and can perhaps me forgiven for appearing to linger over long before sending their opening bats into the fray. In actual fact, it was not they who were slow but we who were astoundingly rapid. Never in the annals of Alcohology fielding can so many overs have been bowled in so short a time. Milsted and Timbers were actually seen to run back to their marks between deliveries. The Berserker was naught but a blur, his features obscured by the acting of g-forces on his face. Concern was expressed for the Maverick’s knees amid such unprecedented hustling. Like super-wound clockwork powered by a spring of adamant, the field changed between overs and for right/left batsmen, so quickly and efficiently it could have been mistaken for an automaton of the silicon age.",
      "Wickets fell. Milsted managed to get enough of his body to function simultaneously to take one caught/bowled and one lbw, finishing with 4-1-2-7, grateful that cod liver oil, WD40 and Ibuprofen are not banned stimulants. Mr Wills attracted interest from the City noise patrol, disturbing roosting birds and triggering Antipodean seismographs with his thunderous grunt, deafening the close field but yielding an lbw. He also attracted the attentions of the local constabulary, who issued him with a speeding ticket for completing his final over inside 45 seconds, in flagrant disregard of the Yorkshire speed-limit of 10 minutes.",
      "By the third or fourth over, however, a look of concern was beginning to manifest itself across Alcohology faces. It took another few overs for this is become apparent, mostly because it was difficult to see each other’s faces. The long shadows of evening were becoming the grotesquely stretched stuff of horror films, with every slight movement of a fielder’s hand or twitch of their hair creating fantastical shadow-puppetry across the pitch. Furthermore, a mist was slowly seeping from the long grass beyond the outfield and creeping around the boundary with an eerie, relentless whisper. From out of the gloom, the Berserker wheeled and called mournfully into the dusk like a lonely cormorant circling in the towering lee of a darkling sea-cliff. His call grew more mournful still, however, when he finally managed to shatter the stumps after a fruitless season of thwarted effort, only for the batsman to look up in sheer amazement that he was involved in a game of cricket and express surprise that he should be called upon to do something about it. Sporting behaviour won the day, and he was encouraged to remain at his post.",
      "As darkness encroached, the Demon and the Tweak assumed responsibilities, both bowling superbly well in difficult conditions. The gathering fog now formed a spectral ring of spectators, and was starting to seep into the trousers of Milsted at third man. Captain Arnott took the ball, bravely offering to set aside his late-onset seaming and revert to the spin of his youth. Then occurred a unique moment: a delivery that never was. No-one saw it. No-one knew what had happened. No-one could honestly say anything had actually happened. And yet the Elfin one no longer had the ball, which was eventually located about 5 yards from the long leg boundary, and nobody could honestly claim that conditions were still suitable for play. Arnott asked mid-off what had happened. Point replied that he didn’t know, and first slip realised he had been facing completely the wrong way. The Heavy Rollers’ captain announced that their innings was declared, and promptly walked into the sightscreen. Finally, by a combination of touch, smell and luck, everyone safely left the field to the thronging vapours.",
      "And so, three years after we lost by default in the dying embers of the Summer, we now won by default as the season ended with the last rays of the setting sun glowing palely in the tree tops. It was the 17th over and we were still 104 runs to the good; barring even such calamity as has befallen us in the past, our defeat was extremely unlikely. Common sense, and it must be added a huge slice of good sportsmanship on the part of our opponents, won the day. They could easily have demanded a point; they probably would rather have not played, but ultimately the spirit of cricket smiled upon us all.",
      "Summer had passed, and Autumn had come to shape our exploits into dreams once more. As the chaps removed their sweaty boots and stowed away their blades for the winter, I remained seated awhile in my deckchair. The ghostly audience had staged something of a pitch-invasion, and was now disporting itself upon the dripping canvas of the wicket-covers as they were shunted into position. The sun had long since dipped below the golf course hedge, casting the last of her light on half a century’s worth of lost balls and the torn threads of a thousand white jumpers mauled in the fruitless effort of retrieval. The north wind, as so often before, seemed to sweep across the outfield for the first time, sending the mist swirling in a dance of remembrance for the season just gone. The year turned before my eyes, and I knew it was all over once again.",
      "And yet, as ever, the fulfilment of the fixture list signalled the beginning of the tall tales, exaggerations and selective memories of the summer, to be told and re-told until none who hears them would doubt that it was the greatest ever, surpassed by none, just as was the year before, and the year before that. And of course, it is all true, as it always has been, as the friendships forged between the wickets become fast and fully tempered by the winter’s yarning.",
      "But there is always a greater season to talk about. One that sets new records, holds new challenges, and promises great adventures. Always the best, always the one that will out-live the rest. The season to come, my friends: next year, as it always has been and always should be, will be even better yet.",
      "Fondest regards,",
      "FWT"
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "gsa",
    "title": "The GSA Match and Robo-Crowe",
    "subtitle": "Trumper returns from hospital to witness a gallant defeat",
    "summary": "Freshly discharged from hospital, Trumper watches ten men (plus the rebuilt Robo-Crowe) fall just short against GSA, undone by a centurion named Holmes.",
    "source": "trumper-GSA.doc",
    "paragraphs": [
      "I woke in a state of some confusion, rising out of a dark dream like a cork bobbing to the surface of a steaming miasmic swamp. At first I could see very little, besides hazy lights and vague suggestions of faces. As if from some great distance, I heard my own voice, weak and tremulous, complain that I could not see.",
      "‘Pop your glasses on, dear’ a familiar voice suggested, as the thickly rimmed spectacles were pushed into my hands and, after an ecstasy of fumbling, helped onto my face like a gas-mask being fitted to a terrified child. I blinked, my vision clearing, and surveyed my surroundings. The colour of the walls, the array of equipment, the strip lighting and the several nurses holding items of my clothing; I suddenly knew where I was.",
      "‘My God, is this Madame Spanky’s?’ I exclaimed, half rising in excitement.",
      "A brief pause ensued, ended by a polite cough and the patient voice of Mrs Trumper.",
      "‘No, dear. This is a hospital. You’ve not been very well.’",
      "Several days later, once I had recovered my senses sufficiently to converse with other people rather than the several delightful young apparitions conjured into being by my medication, I learnt what you all surely knew already, namely that I have been absent for some time. Early last summer, in fact shortly after my belated missive describing our heroics against Chemistry, I suffered a major bout of an old illness. I refer to the hallucinatory malaise into which I periodically lapse, which as I described several years ago in these pages, is the legacy of my inadvertent consumption of the trunk of the species Pachydermus Indus Unitesticulus. This time the attack was a particularly severe one, and I believe I have the local press of a certain southern seaside town to thank for their kind refusal to publish more than cursory details of the strange incident at an end-of-pier show that was witnessed by a party of school children from Dieppe.",
      "I was aghast to learn, as you might well imagine, that I had missed the majority of last summer, and had not witnessed much heroic toiling. Upon leaving hospital, however, I was considerably reassured to find things much as I had left them. The Viking and the Gangler are in fine early season form, the Maverick and the Changonator bristle with intent, and the elfin one, our Captain Marvel, is sitting out the early season with injury. All seems perfectly normal.",
      "From our sedan chairs on the boundary, Captain Arnott and I witnessed a fine game, which was beautifully accompanied by Mrs. Trumper’s excellent hamper. As we got to grips with her marvellous buns and superlative muffin, our chaps took to the field to try and keep the mighty GSA down to something less than 250. I am delighted to report that they managed this in spades, although had they achieved it with spades, we might have stood a better chance of winning the game. The Elfin One has already described many of the details, so I shall instead provide a more impressionist portrayal of events.",
      "Milsted took the only wicket, which coming as it did with the eleventh ball of the match and rendering the Panthers 4-1, represented a suitably Scottish aspect to the situation. Glory was surely ours for certain, and a torrent of optimism spilled from Arnott, requiring the rapid deployment of a napkin. The early promise was, however, soon washed away by a rapidly advancing tide of reality. On this occasion, reality had the name Holmes. 18 overs later, and despite the heroic efforts of all concerned, the receding tide took with it a score of 110 n/o, leaving us beached and bedraggled like so many hermit crabs amid the seaweed.",
      "Do not, however, assume that all went ill. We took to the field with only 10 men, and had the Maverick and the Mallet not persisted with their brand of scintillating swing, the score would surely have been far higher. Had the Daemon not covered half the boundary in his all-encompassing stride, then matters would surely have got out of hand. Had the Neoprene Prince not been at his regal best, the off-side field would have resembled the first evening of the Somme.",
      "Do not study the bowling figures too closely, for they don’t tell the full tale of astonishing bravery in the brutal face of brilliance. They don’t tell how the Maverick, yet another shining example of the true English officer-class to take the Alcohology helm, conjured Mumtaz’s wicket by tempting him with a gap at mid-off, allowing the Gangler to slip the ball through the gate like a hunting knife finding the un-armoured space betwixt breastplate and codpiece. They don’t tell how the Little Master sprang like a kitten on a spring morning to deny our tormentors the luxury of a cheeky flick behind. They don’t tell how close our Viking came to despatching the Panthers to Valhalla, nor how desperately unlucky the Growbag was to face a man in the kind of form most mortals only dream of in our wilder moments.",
      "Through, therefore, a combination of our spirited bowling and skilful fielding, we applied the pressure, and like any top side in any sporting contest, our opponents were harried into errors. Unfortunately, they weren’t harried into very many of them. We faced the total of 160 to win, against a side we have rarely managed a century against between us. It was time to unleash our secret weapon.",
      "I say that we took the field with 10 men. Mid-way through the final over, however, that number increased by one, though some would argue that on sheer species terms, it remained as it had been. From a packing-case placed behind the sight-screen, and still covered in bits of fragmented polystyrene foam, emerged a devastating sight. All but physically destroyed by a long and brilliant career in amateur football and cricket, some thought that John Crowe might retire to bask in the glory of a life in sport few of us could aspire to. But no. Working tirelessly through the winter, a team of engineers and scientists have been busy achieving the impossible. Barely alive, they have re-built him, and so Robo-Crowe took to the field for the final over.",
      "The Panthers quailed, but not audibly. Their innings concluded in what most observers would describe as concealed panic. We prepared to reply to their score with such hope and purpose as we have never had before. Robo-Crowe, his eye-slit glowing red with the light of battle, would open the batting with Captain Maverick. We gathered on the boundary and waited for carnage to ensue.",
      "With a hydraulic hiss and a whiff of WD 40, Robo-Crowe launched into the GSA attack, creaming two fours back over the bowler’s head with the finely engineered effortless perfection one would expect for 6 million dollars. Watching him run another two was like witnessing some crazed cubist dream of an android future. Watching him hole-out on 10 and return, antennae limp and cyber-bat trailing oil behind him, inspired thankfulness that we had witnessed the future of humanity, as much as sorrow for the seeming ending of our cause.",
      "As has been the case so many times before, such thoughts were grossly premature, if eventually fairly accurate.",
      "The Neoprene Prince strode to the wicket, wearing a pair of tight nylon trousers with a natty blue piping that he claims can get Channel 5 if rubbed hard enough. The effect was immediate. Distracted by fuzzy images of Australian soap operas and soft pornography that intermittently flickered across his backside, the GSA fielders were soon diving for cover as our all-American action hero set about the bowling like an MP in a lucky dip. At the other end, Captain Maverick betrayed his nickname with a series of elegant strokes and classical blows that put me in mind of Compton and Cowdrey at their supreme best. The Elfin one and I settled in our chairs and savoured the spectacle.",
      "Under the beady eye of Crowe, the now black-clad Cyborg-Umpire, the Changonator had blitzed his way to 37 by the arrival of the 16th over, taking the team along at the required rate with only another 40 needed for victory. Mitchell was faring even better, having crafted a textbook fifty and supplied intent to score fifty more. For the first time in five years, it seemed that the impossible could be achieved, without recourse to violence. We were in the hunt and on course for a stunning and historic victory.",
      "In such moments, a team looks to its talisman for salvation. How often have we looked to the Viking or the Little Master to rescue us with ball and bat? Many times. It should have come as no surprise, then, that the GSA centurion should transpire to be their hero for hard times. Holmes took the ball, carefully adjusted his field, tested the wind speed and direction, and then ripped out Charno’s off-stump. Three balls later, and the supremely unfortunate Growbag suffered the same fate, exposed once again to the full force of a storm while the rest of us took shelter behind his brave, battered frame and were grateful for his fortitude. Then the Little Master, brogues shining beautifully in the early evening light, did his best to cast his customary spell, only to find the task was beyond any man, even one so stellar a Trojan as he.",
      "Thus it was that our innings closed on 117 for 3, our efforts with ball and bat effectively thwarted by one man experiencing a moment of sporting Elysium. Such is the Corinthian experience of the world; sometimes it is too damn hard, despite one’s best efforts. I must record, however, that our defeat was softened by the sporting and gracious behaviour of our opponents, about whom we have rarely had cause to be impressed. Would it be too much to suppose that the ‘Alcohology way’ of competitive play in a gentlemanly spirit has finally filtered through to even the most hardened of ne’er-do-wells? Perhaps. Maybe it is wiser, and less hubristic, to ascribe the changed demeanour of the Panthers to the workings of the Spirit of Cricket, and to hope that her benevolent and beautiful influence can be brought to bear on others whom we regularly face over a summer of battles in the middle.",
      "Might I also add, as I dip my nib once more in the soothing chroniclers’ ink, that it is very pleasant to be back amongst you all. Here’s hoping for a splendid, dry and heroic summer.",
      "Yours,",
      "FW Trumper."
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "gsa-victory",
    "title": "The Great GSA Victory",
    "subtitle": "Escape from Madame Tussauds, and a one-run triumph",
    "summary": "After months imprisoned in a Madame Tussauds storeroom, Trumper returns in time to see ten men defend 92 and beat GSA by a single run, denying them the title.",
    "source": "trumper-GSA-2.doc",
    "paragraphs": [
      "Chaps,",
      "It is with great relief and pleasure that I find myself once again able to pen a missive to you all, after a summer that can only be described as something of a trial. And, for once, I mean that figuratively and not literally.",
      "I won’t go into great detail about my latest mishap, but I feel some explanation for my silence is required. On a day that I shall forever rue, I found myself in London in the early spring and, full of the joys of that season, and under heavy persuasion from my great-great grand niece, I visited Madame Tussauds. She didn’t accompany me; I realised too late that it was a cunning ruse to get rid of me for an afternoon. Entirely understandable, I suppose; she is a 19 year old professional exotic dancer and crusty old wax-works aren’t really her thing. And she hates Madame Tussauds, too.",
      "Anyway, I ended up in that accursed place and, having paid for the dubious privilege, found myself unwilling to leave before I had had my money’s worth. I soon tired of the company of various world leaders and faded sporting heroes (although I was so startled to encounter the effigy of my old batting partner Hubert R. Spoffing that I greeted it with an effusive ‘Why hello, you randy old bastard!’ before realising my error. Cost me £3.50 in tea in the café to placate the old dear I nearly startled to death in the process). Finding the whole business rather tiring, I settled myself on a chair in a quiet corner and dozed off, intending thereby to restore my energy for an evening at the club.",
      "I awoke with a start and was terrified to find myself staring into the waxwork eyes of Ruby Wax in full demonic grin. I was further alarmed to note that I appeared to be bound nose-to-nose with this horror in a public lavatory. I have woken up in some pretty rum places before, but this was an entirely novel experience. I managed to wriggle free of my bonds and took stock – the lavatory was clearly disused as a place of mictural relief and currently utilised as a store for unwanted figurines. Also present were Frank Boff, David Ike and Tina Turner; words cannot convey the true ghastliness of it all.",
      "I lost track of time during my incarceration, and survived only because the water supply had been left on, and because Mrs Trumper insists on sewing unfeasibly large quantities of Kendal Mint Cake into the lining of all my jackets (for when I have ‘my little mishaps’, as she puts it). Suffice it to say that several months later, the door was unlocked and I was rescued. Apparently, when asleep, I bear an uncanny resemblance to a moth-eaten Edward Heath; I was accordingly tidied away with other redundant models in the lavatory-cum-storeroom until the money could be found to melt us all down for new figurines. Indeed, it was only by luck that I was discovered during waking hours, and thus narrowly avoided being re-modelled as Benjamin Netanyahu.",
      "Thus it was that I managed to miss almost the entire cricket season, a fact I would bemoan to my dying day were it not for the astounding outcome of the one game I did attend, of which more later. I cannot, therefore, relate first-hand how we lost our opener against PDP amid dire mutterings of biased umpiring, nor can I reveal in detail how we won the next match against Psychology with batting performances from Pete ‘Maverick’ Mitchell and Andy ‘Growbag’ Hellewell that set the tone for a season’s prolific stroking and striking. This was the first in a run of four successive victories, all of which occurred during my sojourn in the waxy darkness. I am told that the victory against Biology was notable for three things. Firstly, the splendid performance of the Ganglina, spouse of the Gangler, and a finer bat that many who have taken the field for us in the past. Secondly, the remarkable approach to restricting the opponent’s run-rate, namely sending every fielder bar the ‘keeper to the boundary edge and bowling high-altitude doodle-bugs; this lead to the third element, which was a superlative last-over triple-wicket maiden bowled by Captain Maverick when they need 12 to win; surely a champagne moment contender.",
      "The next match was the defeat of Plonge and, so my source informs me, saw Maverick Mitchell score 58* and the Changonator open his innings with a 4 and a 6 while he got his eye in. The score of 150 was ably defended, with Robin of Loxley bemusing all and sundry with his Sherwood Specials, and Paddy demonstrating a new delivery: a bouncer pitched a yard in front of his own feet that lured the batsman into a moronic waft to mid-wicket. Another champagne moment hopeful, perhaps; I wish I could have seen it.",
      "At one point during my imprisonment, I was woken in the night by a stabbing pain in my foot and a strange sense of great foreboding. For several weeks, I was quite unable to stand properly, but could not explain what had happened. Fortunately, the mysterious injury had healed by the time of my release, and it was only later when I learnt of the Berserker’s damaged ankle ligaments that the possibility of an other-worldly connection with the Scourge of the Humber occurred to me. With great respect, I would settle for an other-worldly connection over a worldly one; the young ladies of Spain would never forgive me.",
      "Thus the team had to soldier on without the Viking for several games. I understand they slaughtered Heavy Rollers, during which the Changonator joined the Boycott club with some disgracefully slow scoring, including one over that only went for 25. This was followed by a narrow loss to Chemistry, where the top order fell for not very many and only Mr Burrows, our new ‘keeper, and Mrs Gangler maintained respectability. At the end of the innings, the Gangler and Ganglina were united at the crease in a moment unparalleled in league history. It lasted 1 ball, during which he ran her out. He held his ground like a man, but I am told looked suitably sorry for it.",
      "Towards the end of my ordeal in the darkness, the game against GSA was called off during the traditional August monsoon, with Timbers gamely up for it despite only having 7 men, all of whom were already half way to the pub. The hiatus allowed for the recovery of the Great Dane, who returned for the loss against Smith and Nephew that was notable for Mr Savine’s elevation to the status of the ACC immortals via a thunderous wristy pull for 4. This shot ranks alongside the sweep favoured by both Dr Tweak and Mr Burrows, and has earned our middle-order stalwart the title of ‘Bendulkar’ from his admiring captain.",
      "And so to the final game, the re-arranged fixture against GSA that they insisted on despite our generous offer of a draw. As fate would have it, the game actually meant something, with their first defeat in 6 years against Biology setting up a scenario whereby only a win against us could secure them the title. Unsurprisingly, they were rather up for it, which made the small matter of our only having 4 men at the start of the game slightly unfortunate. This chronic under-staffing resulted in a new opening partnership of Mitchell and Bendulkar, with the latter to be lauded for his selfless voluntary spirit in the face of adversity. Although he only scored 2, he did so with a dreamy on-drive that was clearly forged in the crucible of the sub-continent, and rumour has it that the IPL are interested. At the other end, Captain Marvel played his usual cool game, fending off Ian Holmes and twatting Gul to the four winds ( I thank Pete himself for this pithy description). His combination of flare and grit bought sufficient time for a further 5 people to arrive, but by now the light was fading with the rapidity that only August in England can achieve. Tim Owens bravely went in at three, followed by the Changonator at four. His demise, for 10, was swiftly followed by Mitchell, Karfoot, Timbers and the Berserker, leaving poor Burrows to hold the fort with a broken knuckle and the Gangler at the other end.",
      "The sight of the Gangler approaching the wicket is usually the signal for all remaining hope to be firmly abandoned, but as he strode out to the middle, a beam of sunlight escaped from the steel grey sky and illuminated the distant gate to the field. There, sunlight glinting off his shining breast-plate and gallantry oozing from his every pore, came Mr Hellewell, with Mrs Hellewell in close attendance holding aloft his standard and bearing his arms. Inspired by this vision of salvation, the Gangler yelled ‘Get your pads on, mate, this won’t last long!’ and settled himself awkwardly at the crease.",
      "I shall refrain from detailed description of what was the usual ugly array of swiping and swishing, and shall instead remark upon the amazing alacrity with which Mr Burrows cajoled, persuaded, encouraged and at times reminded his batting partner to run. By some miracle, this pair took the score from 40 odd to 80 ish, with Burrows advancing into the 30s and the Gangler scoring a remarkable 14. Remarkable because it included a sweep-shot for four, and because he could only claim to have hit 8 of the 14 he was credited with. Fortunately for his average, however, few take his opinion on batting matters very seriously (which, given his habit of taking guard at the non-striker’s end, is not very surprising), and the disinclination of Umpire Owens to signal byes may also have had something to do with it. Fortunately also, the cricketing gods finally smiled, getting the Gangler to accidentally tread on his stumps and allowing Sir Growbag of Hellewell to take to the wicket for the last 4 balls of the match.",
      "Thus it was that, with class restored, Andy and Simon batted out the final over to achieve a score of 92-9. There were contrasting emotions as the players rushed off in the gloom; supreme confidence from GSA and an air of pleasant surprise mingled with stoic fatalism from ACC. We have defended that total before, but not with 10 men …",
      "As if to herald the faint whiff of optimism amid the general air of concern, the light actually improved by a fraction of a lumen. This should have played into GSA’s hands, and indeed looked as if it might do, as both the Berserker and the Gangler found perfectly good deliveries treated with rough disdain. But then their Captain spooned one of the Gangler’s balls into the warm, safe hands of Robin at mid-on and the first signs of a frown could be discerned on their normally ebullient faces. This was the cue for the Viking to unleash his brand of terror, unluckily without reward but successfully suppressing the run-rate. Frustrated, the batsmen began to take risks, and soon another of the Gangler’s balls was nestling safely in a pair of loving hands, this time belonging to Captain Maverick at point.",
      "Success can bring peril, as we have found to our cost on many previous occasions. Mr Holmes came striding to the wicket, and the ACC of years’ past would have quailed. Not today. Despite a flurry of boundaries, the Gangler soon persuaded this Titan of the league to chop into his own stumps, spurred on by Tim ‘Corporal Jones’ Wills’ assertion that ‘They don’t it up ‘em!’ He was right.",
      "Nevertheless, after the Gangler and Viking had done their best, GSA had 40 odd for 3 and 12 overs to score the remaining runs. Their victory seemed an inevitability, foretold by the Gods; William Hill had closed the book, and Dave Lindsay was grimly polishing the league trophy on the boundary, cursing another season’s 2nd place.",
      "They reckoned without the one factor that has driven our side forward from its very inception, during the ‘wilderness years’ when a collective score above 80 was rare and 100 seemed impossible, to our first victories and slow progression up the table. Through the nurturing captaincies of Kenny, Atha and Arnott, to the current administration of Captain Marvel, this side has always possessed a tremendous and unquenchable team spirit, an instinct to play for one another and an unshakable will to survive. Nowhere was this better displayed that when ‘keeper Burrows was forced to hand the gloves to the Changonator as the agony of his shattered digit grew too painful to bear. Rather than retire, as lesser men would, he refused to leave the field and, face set in grim determination, continued to play his part.",
      "Our spirit shone yet, and we were far from out of the game.",
      "Timbers and Mitchell took over the bowling, and the sudden change of pace wreaked havoc. Wills was rightly incandescent when, in contravention of the Laws, common decency and the bloody obvious, a nick behind wasn’t given. The guilty party soon perished, however, thanks to a wickedly flighted Mitchell delivery that was spanked into the Ionosphere, only to fall into the extraordinary extended hand of the Berserker somewhere in the gloom at long-off. Now the massed ranks of the GSA began to mutter and cast their eyes hopefully to the heavens in search of rain. But it didn’t come. Wickets, however, did.",
      "Captain Mitchell took another 2, and Tim Owens also snared a couple, standing at one point on the brink of a brilliant hat-trick. Then Robin ‘Loxley’ Karfoot was unleashed into the action, as the field adopted the Mitchell Formation. Straining their eyes from afar, the fielders witnessed a series of cunningly floated spinning demons that soon accounted for one hapless batsman and brought the final man to the crease. Dot balls were bowled, a few boundaries slipped through, and soon the required runs were in single figures with four overs still to bowl. Confidence sapped from the field as the enormity of the challenge became clear … it was close, but seemingly just not close enough. Another year of plotting and wondering lay before us; another year of finishing 3rd and pondering just what exactly we have to do to beat the buggers. Another bloody year.",
      "And then, in the 17th over, with GSA on 91-9, Robin skipped up, twirled his arm and released the ball. It looped into the murk, spinning a devious and treacherous path through the ether, and pitched outside off-stump. Spitting and growling, it tore off the seam, and deviated from the expected line, spitefully rearing at the helpless batsman who swished, missed, and staggered forward. Eyes ablaze and nylon sports knickers crackling with static, the Changonator pouched the ball and flung it back. The GSA #11 turned, desperation written in his every sinew, and lunged for the line, seemingly losing the race with the ball by the smallest of unbearable margins. The appeal went up, and all looked to the GSA skipper at square leg.",
      "He crouched slightly and squinted. The breath left our lungs, carrying the final syllable of ‘Howzat???!!!’ into the darkening heavens until it faded into the distance. From his position at point, Timbers saw how close it was and never believed it would be given.",
      "And then, unbelievably, it was. The finger was raised, the batsman walked, and the 10 men of Alcohology were transported to the glorious pastures of Elysium. We had won. We had bowled them out with three overs still to go. We had beaten GSA by a single run.",
      "It gladdens me to report that the congratulations from our opponents were fulsome and sincere, and that we left the field as colleagues in the Corinthian brotherhood. And though we finished 3rd yet again, this time we were one place behind GSA and not the customary two. Yes, Chemistry won the league thanks in part to us, and memories returned briefly to injustices during our defeat to them, but thankfully such thoughts were swiftly banished. As the chill mists of autumn swirled once again around the sightscreens and the oak tress where we spend our happy summers, it was with immense pride and gratitude that I beheld our gallant players, and with great relief that I was there and not sharing a lavatory with a facsimile of Ruby Wax.",
      "Be thankful for small mercies. I am planning to supplement my pension and winter fuel allowance with bookings as the Incredible Sulk of Salisbury, and am looking forward immensely to next year, and the possibilities now before this team. We might even manage nets next season …. who knows what we could achieve then?",
      "Yours aye,",
      "FW Trumper"
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "2012-2013",
    "title": "The 2012 & 2013 Seasons",
    "subtitle": "The Remarkable Hat-trick and the 12-a-side friendly",
    "summary": "A two-year retrospective covering the Gangler's hat-trick from three of the worst balls in Alchos history, and the peculiar 12-a-side match against Chemistry.",
    "source": "trumper-FW Trumper 2013.doc",
    "paragraphs": [
      "My dear friends,",
      "So long has it been since I last communicated with you all that I'm sure many of you were wondering if I'd died, and what was to become of my curio cabinet, and in particular the Gentleman's Support worn by WG Grace in his last innings, which I know a few of you covet. Rest assured, I am alive and well, and my antiquarian cricketing memorabilia is safe and, I might add, well insured.",
      "My memories of the last two years are, I'm afraid, slightly hazy, as if I'd spent them in an incoherent alcoholic stupor, which come to think of it I may have. I am fairly sure we played cricket at some point …. or perhaps it was billiards? Or maybe Jenga? Probably it was just Silly Buggers. Anyway, I do recall some moments of note, which I shall share with you now. Please treat them as you would the anecdote of any other mumbling old bastard – smile, nod occasionally, look grave at suitable intervals, and pretend you haven't heard them before.",
      "2012 was exceedingly wet. Previous years were also pretty soggy but 2012 was exceptionally so. We defeated Heavy Rollers, Smith & Nephew and PDP (the latter apparently for the first time ever, a fact I am not convinced of but am unable to verify as my archive has succumbed to moth, along with most of my trousers). We failed to defeat Chemistry, Biology and Plonge. This last match was notable for Mr Wills' surprising disinclination to move when the Gangler mightily heaved the ball a startling 8 ½ inches and galloped madly up the wicket yelling 'Run you useless bastard' or words to that effect. He was met with a suitably withering stare and a raised finger, and the umpire also gave him out.",
      "2012 was also the year of the Remarkable Hat-trick. I say remarkable, not because it occurred during the opening over of the match, which it did, nor because it was produced from the first three deliveries, true though this statement is. It was truly remarkable for having come about from perhaps three of the worst balls in the history of Alchos cricket. Gangler had positioned Herringshaw at fly slip, firmly in the erroneous belief that he was directing the fielder to third man, and then hurled down a disgusting pie that was clipped straight into Liam's startled hands. The Gangler then somehow persuaded the next batsman to cut at a ludicrously somnambulant attempted slower ball, which was slapped to Robin of Loxley at square leg. Robin, with his customary alchemical ability to transform excrement into beauty, took the catch at ankle height and set up the most unlikely sporting feat imaginable.",
      "What happened next can only be explained by psychology. Gangler would claim that the incoming batsman was so terrified at the pace and accuracy on display, that he was simply intimidated into the peculiar skip backwards that saw his middle pole torn from the firmament. What actually transpired was that the batsman was so busy trying to reconcile events with reason that he simply forgot, momentarily, that he was playing cricket. This is the only way that the rank, slow, wayward and eminently thumpable filth that was the Gangler's third ball somehow managed to apologetically sidle up to the stumps and disingenuously dislodge a bail. The score was 0-3 and no-one was quite sure how. The mystery persists to this day.",
      "2013 brought dryer weather and better fortunes. We defeated Chemistry, Plonge, PDP, Heavy Roller and Smith & Nephew, only to be denied the title by a loss to Biology by a single run. Or at least that is what Wisden records; personally I think that's tripe as I'm sure we lost at least one other match. However, I had a bad reaction to mixing sherry with some charming mushrooms that my grand-niece found in the garden and spent much of June firmly in the belief that I was the progeny of the Dalai Lama and a cabbage. Consequently I fear my recollection of the summer may lack a degree of accuracy. I do recall, however, that this year was notable for Mr Millward and Mr Hellewell's renewed ability to take wickets, and for Mr Charno's rediscovered confidence at the crease. Particularly satisfying was the long-awaited flowering of Bendulkar, whose promotion to opener inspired some thrilling shots and glorious strokes. This was also the year that our wicket keeper took more wickets that our opening bowler, a feat perhaps unmatched in the annals of the game and worthy of mention.",
      "What is also not in doubt is that the match against Chemistry must go down as perhaps the most peculiar in the history of the game. Unusually, we had managed to scrape together 11 players, which was 6 more than we were lead to believe that the opposition had. Being erstwhile honest Corinthians to a man, we couldn't be arsed to rearrange the fixture and offered to provide some players. Tim gallantly rustled up 5 extras and was understandably confused to find that Chemistry actually have 10 after all. His sterling recruitment efforts were recognised and applauded by all and it was sensibly decided to play a 12-aside friendly.",
      "Still reeling from the suspect mathematics of that equation, we were even more surprised when, following the outcome of the toss (which, regrettably, I forget), Chemistry asked us to field and then unveiled their new opening bat … none other then Mr Wills. A stunned silence fell across the field as he strode to the crease like Bradman in his prime and confidently took guard. Unable to fathom what was going on, we responded in kind. So it was that Gary has the unique honour to have delivered his first ball to a slip cordon of 10, strung out in a vast parabola across half the field.",
      "What transpired next was an example of cricketing theatre at its purest. The Goole Berserker thundered in, leaping like an aroused gazelle and firing down a life-threateningly rapid grenade. Tim fixed the delivery with the cold steely gaze of the Terminator and lifted his bat. The cordon held it's unusually capacious breath, 20 hands rising to receive the benefaction of the edge. The ball pitched and fizzed off the turf, leaving a distinct scorch mark just short of a length. Tim's hands moved inexorably towards the line of the ball and the cordon collectively squeezed its buttocks in anticipation. And then, majestically, just as the ball was seemingly about to kiss the bat and fly to its doom, Tim gloriously demonstrated the value of years of vigorous and prolonged wrist exercises and withdrew his blade at the critical moment. Thwarted, the ball zipped past, harmlessly nestling in Burrow's disappointed gloves.",
      "What made things worse was that the very next delivery, after the field had been re-organised to more resemble something approximating MCC regulations, Tim firmly edged the ball, which flew safely through the recently vacated 9th slip and ran away to the boundary for 4. Mr Wills went on to make 33, but is forgiven, partly because his apparent Judas-impression was in fact born of charity, and mostly because we won so it didn't matter. This was also the match, I have been firmly instructed to remind you all, where Mrs Gangler took a lovely catch, thus perhaps making up in some way for the one she took almost 10 years ago, which resulted in her becoming Mrs Gangler.",
      "And so, my friends, as the Autumnal mists draw once again across the field, and the wicket beds down beneath its quilt of dew-soaked cobwebs, we bid farewell to another year of comradeship, endeavour, bravery and joy. I regret most bitterly that I could not be with you tonight, but rest assured that WG Grace's box has been topped up, and is now raised in your honour. Your health, good friends!",
      "Yours,",
      "FW Trumper."
    ]
  }
];
